From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:19:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Enable the CPU idle node

Enable the T8132 CPU idle node on the MacBook Pro J604, as
commit 3ef17de48d03 ("arm64: dts: apple: t8132: Add the CPU idle node
and enable it on J713") does for the J713. The node carries no
board values: "apple,t8132-cpuidle" makes cpuidle-apple run both of
its states through WFI, because iBoot locks CYC_OVRD on the M4 SoCs.

The J604 is the same SoC as the J713 and idles its cores the same way.
In the 26B123 j604ap and j713ap device trees every one of the ten
/cpus/cpuN nodes has function-cpu_idle pointing at the PMGR (cpuI),
and /arm-io/pmgr has the same cpu-apsc (1) and
cpu-power-gate-latency-us (50000) on both. The J604's own boot ADT
under the 25G83 stub matches.

Without a cpuidle driver, suspend-to-idle never reaches
cpuidle_enter_s2idle(), so the tick is never frozen and timekeeping is
never suspended. Normal idle stays WFI.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index e8a773994a..ce2afdbc25 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -449,6 +449,10 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+&cpuidle {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 /*
  * CPU thermal policy: one zone on the SMC's hottest CPU die reading (TCMz)
  * throttles both clusters through cpufreq. While it works, the T8132 cpufreq
